mm in members:\n        members[comm].sort()\n    # Aggregate edges\n    edge_dict = {}\n    for u, v, w in edges:\n        cu = refinement[u]\n        cv = refinement[v]\n        # canonical order\n        if cu > cv:\n            cu, cv = cv, cu\n        key = (cu, cv)\n        edge_dict[key] = edge_dict.get(key, 0) + w\n    # Convert to sorted list\n    sorted_edges = sorted([list(k) + [v] for k, v in edge_dict.items()])\n    return {\"aggregate_edges\": sorted_edges, \"members\": members}\n\nNote: Output schema has additionalProperties false, but we are only returning those two keys, fine.\n\nNow put as JSON string. Use double quotes, escape backslashes, newlines as \\n. Alternatively use raw string and replace. Simpler: use json.dumps on the code string, but we need to output the code as a string. Since we are writing the response directly, we can construct the JSON manually.\n\nBetter: write the code as a single line with escaped newlines. Or use triple quotes and replace newlines. We'll do:\n\nimplementation_string = \"def solve(case):\\n    edges = case['edges']\\n    refinement = case['refinement']\\n    members = {}\\n    for node, community in refinement.items():\\n        members.setdefault(community, []).append(node)\\n    for comm in members:\\n        members[comm].sort()\\n    edge_dict = {}\\n    for u, v, w in edges:\\n        cu = refinement[u]\\n        cv = refinement[v]\\n        if cu > cv:\\n            cu, cv = cv, cu\\n        key = (cu, cv)\\n        edge_dict[key] = edge_dict.get(key, 0) + w\\n    sorted_edges = sorted([list(k) + [v] for k, v in edge_dict.items()])\\n    return {'aggregate_edges': sorted_edges, 'members': members}\"\n\nBut careful: the code uses single quotes for dictionary keys; we need to ensure it's valid Python. It's fine.\n\nNow we need to embed this into JSON. We'll produce:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    edges = case['edges']\\n    ...\" }\n\nBut inside JSON, we must escape the backslashes: \\n be